    <stereotype stereotypeDefinitionId="14441454-bfe0-4d5d-8dba-d86d74d33a3b" name="AI Settings">
      <addedByDefault>false</addedByDefault>
      <definitionPackageName>Intent.ModuleBuilder</definitionPackageName>
      <definitionPackageId>9972b2a9-b749-4bba-b5c8-824bf694c6ef</definitionPackageId>
      <properties>
        <property name="17477a69-d9a5-4251-9329-0963e8121b5f" display="Rules" value="## Foreign Key Generation for 1-1 (non-collection) Associations&#xA;&#xA;When `Key Creation Mode` is `Explicit` (the default), a Foreign Key attribute is only generated automatically for a non-collection (1-to-1 style) association when the association's **source** end multiplicity is `0..1`. The target end's multiplicity only controls whether the generated FK is nullable — it does NOT determine whether an FK gets created at all.&#xA;&#xA;This means direction matters: whether an FK attribute appears is decided by which class you modeled as the association's *source*, not just by which end looks &quot;optional&quot;. To get an FK on a specific class, create the association FROM that class TO the class it references, with that class's own (source) end set to `0..1`.&#xA;&#xA;If you author the association the other way around (source end `1`, target end `0..1` or `1`), NO Foreign Key attribute will be created on either class — and no validation error or warning will indicate this. This is easy to fall into: e.g. modeling &quot;Customer has zero-or-one Passport; Passport requires exactly one Customer&quot; the natural way (Customer as source/`1`, Passport as target/`0..1`) produces no FK anywhere.&#xA;&#xA;If you need the FK on the &quot;wrong&quot; side per this rule, or both ends must be mandatory (`1` -&gt; `1`), the automatic rule cannot produce it. In that case, switch the `Key Creation Mode` application setting to &quot;Manually add PKs and FKs&quot;, then add the Attribute yourself and apply the `Foreign Key` stereotype (setting its `Association` property to the correct association end). Note: while `Key Creation Mode` remains `Explicit`, a manually-added attribute for a non-qualifying association will be silently deleted the next time the class is modified, because the automatic rule still does not recognize it as required." isActive="true" />
      </properties>
    </stereotype>
  </stereotypes>
